# Copy to .env NEXT TO compose.yml on the Pi and edit.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && vi .env && docker compose up -d

# ── The public name ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The hostname you route to this tunnel in the Cloudflare dashboard. Cloudflare
# holds the certificate and terminates TLS; the app only needs to be told what
# name it is being served under. No DNS record to create by hand — adding the
# public hostname to the tunnel writes the CNAME for you.
DOMAIN=packripper.example.com

# ── The tunnel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Zero Trust dashboard → Networks → Tunnels → Create a tunnel → Cloudflared. The
# token is in the install command it shows you, after `--token`. It is a
# credential for your account: treat it like a password, and note .env is
# gitignored for exactly this reason.
#
# Setting this is also the switch that starts cloudflared inside the app's
# container. When you add the public hostname to the tunnel, the service to
# point it at is http://localhost:3000.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# ── Where the data lives ───────────────────────────────────────
# Default is beside this file. If the Pi boots from an SD card, point at least
# DATA_DIR at an SSD: every mutation rewrites the entire database file, so an
# evening of slots is gigabytes of writes to one spot on a card that has no
# wear-levelling worth the name.
#DATA_DIR=/mnt/ssd/packripper/.data
#CACHE_DIR=/mnt/ssd/packripper/.cache

# ── Which build to run ─────────────────────────────────────────
# `latest` follows main. Pin the immutable sha- tag from the build's Actions
# summary to know exactly what is running and make a rollback one line.
TAG=latest

# ── Admin ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Empty means the door is OFF rather than unlocked. A fresh database has no
# admins and nothing in the UI can promote the first one.
#ADMIN_USERNAMES=travis

# Generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#ADMIN_TOKEN=

# ── Hourly backups to a Nextcloud ──────────────────────────────
# Read by backup.sh, which install.sh runs on an hourly systemd timer. Compose
# ignores all four. Leave NEXTCLOUD_URL empty and backups are simply off.
#
# The URL is the server root and nothing more — no /index.php, no /apps/files.
# The password should be an app password (Nextcloud → Settings → Security →
# Devices & sessions), not your login password: it is stored in plain text here,
# and an app password can be revoked on its own.
#NEXTCLOUD_URL=https://cloud.example.com
#NEXTCLOUD_USER=you
#NEXTCLOUD_PASS=
#NEXTCLOUD_PATH=PackRipper

# ── Misc ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BODY_SIZE_LIMIT=2M
